 ## Overview
-Raymarching with LOD-based Brick Caching is a visualization method for Compressed Segmentation Volumes (CSVs) that renders volumes by decompressing bricks on-demand at the required Level of Detail (LOD) and storing them in a GPU cache [S1]. It solves the problem of rendering extremely large segmentation volumes by exploiting temporal coherency and detail separation, allowing coarse LOD data to remain on the GPU while fine LOD data is streamed from CPU memory [S1]. It differs from its siblings by using a specific cache structure of contiguous base elements rather than superblocks/columns, and by testing palettes for empty-space skipping before requesting cache locations [S1].
+Raymarching with LOD-based Brick Caching is a visualization method for Compressed Segmentation Volumes (CSVs) that renders volumes by decompressing bricks on-demand at the required Level of Detail (LOD) and storing them in a GPU cache [S1]. It solves the problem of efficiently rendering massive segmentation datasets by exploiting temporal coherency and empty-space skipping, allowing a one-frame lag for decompression requests to decouple rendering speed from data decompression [S1]. It differs from Shading Atlas Streaming (SAS) by testing bricks for empty space using palettes before requesting cache locations, omitting superblock/column concepts in favor of contiguous base elements, and decompressing bricks during location assignment [S1].
 
 ## How it works
-**Inputs:** Compressed Segmentation Volume (CSV) data (palette and compressed voxel data), Transfer Function (TF), camera position and orientation [S1].
-**Outputs:** Rendered image [S1].
