 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** Raymarching is $O(P)$ where $P$ is pixels [G]. Cache assignment is $O(B)$ where $B$ is bricks [G]. Decompression is parallel on GPU [S1].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Depends on cache size and detail separation [G]. For the CORTEX dataset (6144x9216x4096), L0 (finest) compressed data is 9.2 GB, while all other levels take 4.3 GB [S1].
-*   **Performance:** The method allows rendering of very large CSVs by keeping coarse data on GPU and streaming fine data [S1]. The 8 MB upload buffer is sufficient in most frames [S1].
-*   **Strengths:** Exploits temporal coherency, efficient empty-space skipping, handles large datasets via detail separation [S1].
-*   **Limitations:** One-frame lag for LOD changes and streaming [S1]. Cache rebuilds may cause stutter if fragmentation is high (though rare with reasonably sized pools) [S1].
+*   **Time Complexity:** Decompression is performed in parallel on the GPU. Cache assignment and eviction are linear with respect to the number of visible bricks [S1].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Depends on the cache size and the separation of coarse/fine LOD data. For the CORTEX dataset (6144x9216x4096 voxels), memory consumption for L0 (finest detail) is 9.2 GB, while all other levels take 4.3 GB [S1].
+*   **Performance:** Efficiently handles large CSVs by streaming fine detail levels only when required [S1].
+*   **Strengths:** Exploits temporal coherency, empty-space skipping, and LOD-based caching to reduce memory bandwidth and decompression overhead [S1].
+*   **Limitations:** One-frame lag for decompression may cause visual artifacts during rapid camera movement [S1]. Cache rebuilds, though rare, can cause stuttering [S1].
 
